<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened runtime entitlements for Galactus.

  The hardened runtime is already on (tauri.conf.json, bundle.macOS.hardenedRuntime).
  It closes a fixed list of doors; an entitlement reopens exactly one of them.
  Every key below is here because a door the product actually walks through is
  closed by default. Nothing else is granted, and the empty set is the default:
  a capability that works today under the hardened runtime does not get a key
  "to be safe", because a granted entitlement is a permanently widened attack
  surface that reviewers have to justify forever.

  GRANTED

    com.apple.security.device.audio-input
      Galactus dictates locally. Resources/packaged/galactus-voice opens
      AVAudioEngine on the built in microphone and feeds SFSpeechRecognizer.
      Under the hardened runtime a process that calls AVCaptureDevice for
      .audio without this key is denied, and the denial is silent from the
      user's point of view. The app itself carries the key as well as the
      helper because the app is the TCC responsible process: it is Galactus,
      not the helper, that owns the microphone grant and whose Info.plist
      NSMicrophoneUsageDescription is shown in the prompt.

  DELIBERATELY NOT GRANTED, with the reason

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      Nothing in the process JIT compiles. The web view runs its JavaScript in
      WebKit's own out of process WebContent service, which carries its own
      signature and its own entitlements; the host app never needs them. The
      Metal shaders of the inference engine are compiled by MTLCompilerService
      over XPC, not as executable pages in llama-server.

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
      Every Mach-O inside the bundle is signed by the same Developer ID team
      before the bundle is sealed (scripts/macos-sign-resources.sh), so library
      validation is satisfied without disabling it. The app loads no plugin it
      did not ship: there is no dlopen anywhere in src-tauri/src.

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
      No self modifying code, no injected loader environment.

    com.apple.security.automation.apple-events
      The app spawns /usr/bin/osascript for `choose folder` and
      `display notification`. Both are Standard Additions commands that
      osascript executes in its own process; no Apple event is sent to another
      application, so the sender entitlement is not needed. The two call sites
      are pick_folder and notify in src-tauri/src/lib.rs.

    com.apple.security.app-sandbox and the com.apple.security.network.*,
    com.apple.security.files.* families
      Those keys only mean something under the App Sandbox, which this build
      does not enable and does not need: Developer ID distribution outside the
      Mac App Store has no sandbox requirement. Binding a TCP listener on
      127.0.0.1 (src-tauri/src/relay.rs), opening a pseudo terminal with
      posix_openpt (src-tauri/src/pty.rs) and running llama-server as a child
      process are all unrestricted under the hardened runtime alone.

    com.apple.security.get-task-allow
      A debugging entitlement. Its presence makes the notary service reject the
      submission. scripts/macos-notarize.sh asserts that it is absent.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
